%======================================================================
\section{Why the switch and fuse sit on live}

\subsection{The danger of switching the neutral}
Suppose a switch is placed in the \textbf{neutral} only. When ``off'', the appliance
is disconnected from the return path --- but the live wire still reaches the
heating element / metal parts. Touching an ``off'' appliance can still give a
shock. If the switch is in the \textbf{live}, opening it isolates the appliance from
high potential.

\subsection{Why the fuse must be on live}
A fuse is a thin wire that melts when $I$ exceeds its rating.
\begin{itemize}
  \item On \textbf{live}: a short to earth or neutral draws a huge current
        \emph{through the fuse} $\Rightarrow$ fuse blows $\Rightarrow$ live is cut.
  \item On \textbf{neutral}: a live-to-case fault can still leave the case at high
        potential even after the fuse melts, or the fault current may not pass
        through the fuse at all, depending on the fault path.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Simple domestic circuit (L / N / E)}
