\worked{2024}{31}{A}{45}{../2024_q31.png}{%
(1)~True: fuse (and switch) belong on the live so that blowing/opening isolates
high potential.
(2)~True: earth is a safety conductor, not part of the working L--N loop; the
appliance still runs without it (but is unsafe if a live-to-case fault occurs).
(3)~False: a fuse protects against \textbf{excessive current} (fire / cable damage).
It does \emph{not} by itself stop a person receiving a shock from a live case ---
that requires earthing (large fault current $\to$ fuse blows) and/or an ELCB/RCD.
$\Rightarrow$ \textbf{A}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Ring main and lighting circuits}

\subsection{Why parallel, not series}
Every appliance needs the full $220\,\mathrm{V}$ and must be independently
switchable. Domestic sockets and lamps are therefore connected in
\textbf{parallel} across L and N.
Adding more appliances \emph{decreases} the equivalent resistance of the circuit
and \emph{increases} the total current drawn from the supply.

\subsection{Ring main (sockets)}
In a \textbf{ring main}, the live (and separately the neutral) forms a loop from
the consumer unit around the sockets and back. Advantages:
\begin{itemize}
  \item each socket is fed by \emph{two} paths, so cable current is shared;
  \item thinner cable can be used for a given total load than a long radial spur;
  \item typical HK/UK ring is protected by a $\sim 30\,\mathrm{A}$ fuse/MCB;
        individual plugs still carry their own (e.g.\ 13~A) fuse.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Lighting circuit}
Lighting is usually a separate \textbf{radial} circuit with a smaller fuse/MCB
(often $5\,\mathrm{A}$ or $6\,\mathrm{A}$), because lamps draw far less current
than heaters or air-conditioners. Switches are on the live feed to each lamp.

\worked{2023}{28}{C}{30}{../2023_q28.png}{%
(1)~True: a.c.\ means live polarity reverses relative to neutral each half-cycle.
(2)~False: the kW\,h meter measures \textbf{energy}, not power.
(3)~True: appliances are in parallel; more branches $\Rightarrow$ smaller $R_{\mathrm{eq}}$.
$\Rightarrow$ \textbf{C}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Short circuit versus overload}

\subsection{Definitions from current paths}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Short circuit:} a very low-resistance accidental path between points
        at different potential (typically L--N or L--E), bypassing the appliance.
        Current becomes huge almost instantly $\Rightarrow$ fuse/MCB must break.
  \item \textbf{Overload:} too many appliances (or one oversized load) drawing a
        current larger than the cable/fuse rating for a sustained time, without a
        near-zero resistance fault. Heating builds up gradually.
\end{itemize}
Both can blow a fuse; a short circuit is more violent and immediate.

\subsection{Which shorts blow the fuse?}
Consider points on L, N, and E after the fuse on live:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{L shorted to N}: current rushes L $\to$ short $\to$ N through the fuse
        $\Rightarrow$ blows.
  \item \textbf{L shorted to E}: current rushes L $\to$ case/earth through the fuse
        $\Rightarrow$ blows (this is how earthing protects you).
  \item \textbf{N shorted to E}: both near $0\,\mathrm{V}$; little extra current through
        the live fuse $\Rightarrow$ fuse usually does \textbf{not} blow
        (but it is still a fault to be repaired).
\end{itemize}

\worked{2012}{33}{C}{40}{../2012_q33.png}{%
$X$ is on neutral, $Y$ on live (after the fuse), $Z$ on earth.
(1)~$X$--$Y$: live shorted to neutral $\Rightarrow$ huge current through fuse $\Rightarrow$ blows.
(2)~$Y$--$Z$: live shorted to earth $\Rightarrow$ huge current through fuse $\Rightarrow$ blows.
(3)~$X$--$Z$: neutral to earth; no large p.d.\ to drive fault current through the
live fuse $\Rightarrow$ does not blow.
$\Rightarrow$ \textbf{C}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Fuse, MCB, and ELCB}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{9.2cm}@{}}
\toprule
Device & What it does \\
\midrule
\textbf{Fuse} &
  Sacrificial thin wire; melts on overcurrent. Cheap, one-shot; must be replaced
  with the \emph{correct} rating (never a larger fuse ``to stop nuisance blowing''). \\
\textbf{MCB}
  (miniature circuit breaker) &
  Electromagnetic/thermal switch that trips on overcurrent; can be reset.
  Same job as a fuse (overcurrent / short / overload), reusable. \\
\textbf{ELCB / RCD}
  (earth leakage / residual current) &
  Compares current in live and neutral. If they differ (leakage to earth through
  a person or damp path), it trips \emph{even if the current is only tens of mA}
  --- far below what would blow a 13~A fuse. Protects against shock. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{Choosing a fuse rating.}
Operating current $I=P/V$ must be \textbf{just below} the fuse rating.
Example: $1760\,\mathrm{W}$ at $220\,\mathrm{V}$ gives $I=8\,\mathrm{A}$ --- choose
a $10\,\mathrm{A}$ or $13\,\mathrm{A}$ fuse, not $5\,\mathrm{A}$ (nuisance) and not
$30\,\mathrm{A}$ (useless protection). Cable rating must exceed the maximum
expected circuit current.
\end{mdframed}

%======================================================================
\section{Double insulation}

Some appliances (plastic hairdryers, phone chargers, \ldots) have
\textbf{no earth pin} (or a plastic earth pin that does not connect).
They rely on \textbf{double insulation}: two independent layers of insulation
so that a single failure cannot make an accessible part live.
Symbol: a square inside a square.
Class~I appliances (metal case) need an earth connection; Class~II are
double-insulated.

%======================================================================
\section{Appliance ratings versus applied voltage}

\subsection{What the rating plate means}
A lamp marked ``$220\,\mathrm{V}$,\,$100\,\mathrm{W}$'' means:
\begin{itemize}
  \item designed to run at $V_{\mathrm{r}}=220\,\mathrm{V}$;
  \item then it dissipates $P_{\mathrm{r}}=100\,\mathrm{W}$;
  \item its resistance (hot) is approximately
        $R=V_{\mathrm{r}}^{2}/P_{\mathrm{r}}$.
\end{itemize}
If you apply a different voltage $V$ (assume $R$ unchanged),
\[
P=\frac{V^{2}}{R}=P_{\mathrm{r}}\left(\frac{V}{V_{\mathrm{r}}}\right)^{2},\qquad
I=\frac{V}{R}.
\]
Halving $V$ quarters the power and halves the current --- heating takes four times
as long for the same energy.

\begin{mdframed}[style=misbox]
\noindent\textbf{Common slip.}
Treating rated power as fixed regardless of supply voltage.
Power changes with $V^{2}/R$ unless the device actively regulates.
\end{mdframed}

